<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#castizo">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castizo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as castiza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castizo (feminine castiza) was a racial category of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> for people of three-quarters Spanish and one-quarter Amerindian ancestry, the child of a Spaniard and a mestiza. The category was widely recognised in colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> by the 18th century and was a standard subject of the century's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casta">casta</a> paintings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Censuses</a> kept records of castizos, in the hope of regulating entry into the coveted español category. Most scholars do not regard the labels as a rigid system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, since people could be classified differently at different points in their lives. In the 1778 census of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, castizos were counted as whites.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Birth to Spanish parents made a criollo, but so, in practice, could wealth and a Spanish way of life: the child of a <b>castizo</b> and a Spaniard counted as Spanish, and prosperous families paid to obscure a mixed ancestry.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
